{"number": 67, "text": "               Occuriu\n              Stress       in    compound             words\n\n              Compound    words  are made  from two  smaller words put together, for example book + shop\n              = bookshop.  (They  are not always written as one word, for example shoe shop.) In most\n              compound   words,  the stress is on the first part. For example, the word bookshop has two\n              syllables and the stress is on the first syllable. Listen to these examples.\n            2 Oo       bookshop,  bus stop, footpath, airport, shoe shop, road sign, car park, bedroom\n              Ooo      traffic light, bus station, sunglasses, boarding card, window seat, check-in desk\n              Oooo    travel agent, art gallery, supermarket, tape recorder, photocopy\n\n              Note: If the first part of the compound word is an adjective, there may be stress on the second part too,\n              for example OO double room.\n\n              Note: There may be stress on the second part of a compound noun when:\n              e the object in the second part is made out of the material in the first, for example OO glass jar,\n              e the first part tells us where the second part is, for example 00 car door.\n\n              If the compound  word  is    A noun, we  often put stress on the second part too.\n              T iotan ta thaca avamnlac\n         ‘B43:\n              OO      first class, half price, hand made\n              OOo     bad-tempered,  old-fashioned, short-sighted\n              OoO   © overnight, second hand\n\n              Sometimes  A compound   word  looks the same as\n              e anormal  adjective and noun,\n              e anormal  noun  and verb.\n              But the pronunciation is different. Compare:\n              | 00 compound  word                              OO adjective and noun                       |\n               We keep these plants in a greenhouse during     Mr Olsen lives in a small, green house\n               the winter months.                              next to the river.\n\n                                                                                                             4\n               00 compound   word                             OO  noun  and verb                          _|\n               | saw her bus pass.                             | saw her bus pass.\n\n              1                                             2\n\n\n    66\n                English Pronunciation in Use", "char_count": 2476, "mean_conf": 89.84, "word_count": 312, "words": [{"text": "66", "x0": 74, "y0": 3016, "x1": 122, "y1": 3050, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Stress", "x0": 284, "y0": 127, "x1": 503, "y1": 193, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "in", "x0": 543, "y0": 128, "x1": 603, "y1": 193, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "compound", "x0": 642, "y0": 130, "x1": 1031, "y1": 214, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "words", "x0": 1070, "y0": 131, "x1": 1288, "y1": 196, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "Compound", "x0": 284, "y0": 276, "x1": 502, "y1": 323, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "words", "x0": 517, "y0": 276, "x1": 635, "y1": 313, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "are", "x0": 651, "y0": 290, "x1": 709, "y1": 314, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "made", "x0": 723, "y0": 277, "x1": 827, "y1": 314, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "from", "x0": 842, "y0": 276, "x1": 935, "y1": 314, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "two", "x0": 950, "y0": 287, "x1": 1023, "y1": 314, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "smaller", "x0": 1039, "y0": 277, "x1": 1176, "y1": 315, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "words", "x0": 1190, "y0": 278, "x1": 1309, "y1": 316, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "put", "x0": 1324, "y0": 289, "x1": 1388, "y1": 325, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "together,", "x0": 1402, "y0": 279, "x1": 1566, "y1": 326, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "for", "x0": 158